= Delving into BFV encryption
\
As you've probably read before, we'll be implementing the BFV (Brakerski-Fan-Vercauteren) encryption scheme in this project.
It is one of the most used schemes in the FHE (Fully Homomorphic encryption) domain.
== 1 - Mathematical basics
\
As we said, BFV is based on the RLWE (Ring learning with errors) problem. The system's security depends on the difficulty to resolve equations in Euclidean lattices.
\
BFV doesn't work over individual bits, but over polynomials. It enables parallelism#footnote[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_instruction,_multiple_data]. We define the "polynomials" ring as :

#align(center)[$R = ZZ[X] slash (X^n+1)$]
Where $n$ is a power of 2, every polynomial in R has a maximum degree of $n - 1$, and the polynomial multiplication is done with modulo $(X^n + 1)$,
as $X^n eq.triple -1$.
\
For encryption, we work with the quotient ring $R_q$ defined as :
\
#align(center)[$R_q = R slash (q*R) = ZZ_q [X] slash (X^n + 1)$]
All quotients are reduced modulo $q$ (a really big unsigned integer), so that they are in the set ${0, 1, ..., q- 1 }$.
\
Finally, for "plaintext" data, we define the ring :
\
#align(center)[$R_t = ZZ_t [X] slash (X^N + 1)$]
Where $t$ is the "message" or "plaintext" modulus (for example $t = 2$ for binary or $t = 256$ for UTF-8 encoding). There is one critical condition : $t << q$ ($t$ needs to be *much* less than $q$).


